WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.779
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.779 --> 00:00:06.360
Oh my goodness.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.750
This work is history in the making.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:16.090
Today we are going to have a look at AlphaFold,
perhaps one of the most important papers of

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:17.930
the last few years.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:24.289
And you will see that nothing that came before
even comes close to it, and that it truly

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:26.450
is a gift to humanity.

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:29.570
So, what is AlphaFold?

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:35.219
AlphaFold is an AI that is capable of solving
protein structure prediction, which we will

00:00:35.219 --> 00:00:38.089
refer to as protein folding.

00:00:38.089 --> 00:00:42.840
Okay…but what is a protein and why does
it need folding?

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:49.660
A protein is a string of amino acids, these
are the building blocks of life.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:54.719
This is what goes in, which in reality, has
a 3D structure.

00:00:54.719 --> 00:00:57.070
And that is protein folding.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:01.030
Letters go in, a 3D object comes out.

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:03.180
This is hard.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.339
How hard exactly?

00:01:04.339 --> 00:01:11.159
Well, let’s compare it to DeepMind’s amazing
previous projects, and we’ll see that none

00:01:11.159 --> 00:01:15.030
of these projects even come close in difficulty.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:19.560
For instance, DeepMind’s previous AI learned
to play chess.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:25.670
Now, why does this matter, as we already have
Deep Blue, which is a chess computer that

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:33.450
can play at the very least as well as Kasparov
did, and it was built in 1995.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:36.270
So, why is chess interesting?

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:40.870
The space of possible moves is huge.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:48.409
And Deep Blue in 1995 was not an AI in the
strictest sense, but a handcrafted technique.

00:01:48.409 --> 00:01:52.590
This means that it can play chess and that’s
it.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.830
One algorithm, one game.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:59.229
If you want a different game, you write a
different algorithm.

00:01:59.229 --> 00:02:02.979
And, yes, that is the key difference.

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:09.730
DeepMind’s Chess AI is a general learning
algorithm that can learn many games, for instance,

00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:12.670
Japanese chess, Shogi too.

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:15.050
One algorithm, many games.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:18.020
And, yes, chess is hard.

00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:20.890
But these days, the AI can manage.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.590
Then, Go is the next level.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:27.840
This is not just hard, it is really hard.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:33.880
The space of possible moves is significantly
bigger, and we can’t just evaluate all the

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:39.500
long-term effects of our moves, it is even
more hopeless than chess, and that’s often

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:44.690
why people say that this game requires some
sort of intuition to play.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:52.550
But DeepMind’s AI solved that too and beat
the world champion Go player 4 to 1 in a huge

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:54.090
media event.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:56.300
The AI can still manage.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:03.620
Now, get this, if Chess is hard, and Go is
very hard, then protein folding is sinfully

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:04.620
difficult.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:12.130
Once again, a string of text encoding the
amino acids go in, a 3D structure comes out.

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:13.640
Why is this hard?

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:18.760
Why not just try every possible 3D structure
and see what sticks?

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:20.570
Well, not quite.

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:26.770
The search space for this problem is still
stupendously large, perhaps not as big as

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:33.410
playing a continuous strategy game like Starcraft
2, but the search here is much less forgiving.

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:39.400
Also, we don’t have access to a perfect
scoring function, so it is very difficult

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:43.200
to define what exactly should be learned.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:49.910
In a strategy game, a win is a win, but for
proteins, nature doesn’t really tell us

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:53.020
what it is up to when creating these structures.

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:59.600
Thus, DeepMind did very well in Chess and
Go, and Starcraft too, and challenging as

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:05.480
they are, they are not even close to being
as challenging as protein folding.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:06.730
Not even close.

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:10.440
To demonstrate that, look, this is CASP.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:16.570
I've heard DeepMind CEO Demis Hassabis call
it the Olympics of protein folding.

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:21.900
If you look at how teams of scientists prepare
for this event, you will probably agree that

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:26.010
yes, this is indeed the Olympics of protein
folding.

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:33.090
At about a score of 90, we can think of protein
folding as a mostly solved problem.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:39.170
No need to worry about definitions though,
look, we are not even close to 90.

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:41.670
And it gets even worse, look.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:48.800
This GDT score means the global distance test,
this is a measure of similarity between the

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.930
predicted and the real protein structure.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:55.090
And, wait a second.

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:56.090
What?

00:04:56.090 --> 00:05:01.840
The results are not only not too good, but
they appear to get worse over time.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.100
Is that true?

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:04.790
What is going on here?

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:07.880
Well, there is an explanation.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:14.030
The competition gets a little harder over
time, so even flat results mean that there

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:17.470
is a little improvement over time.

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:22.980
And now, hold on to your papers, and let’s
look at the results from DeepMind’s AI-based

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:24.570
solution, AlphaFold.

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:27.760
Wow, now we’re talking.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.050
Look at that!

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:34.580
The competition gets harder, and it is not
only flat, but can that be?

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:38.340
It is even better than the previous methods.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:43.170
But, we are not done here, no-no, not even
close.

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:48.639
If you have been holding on to your papers,
now, squeeze that paper, because what you

00:05:48.639 --> 00:05:51.120
see here is old news.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:56.900
Because only two years later Alphafold 2 appeared.

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:58.720
And just look at that.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:01.030
It came in guns blazing.

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:06.170
So much so that the result is…I can’t
believe it!

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:09.150
It is around the 90 mark.

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:13.270
My goodness, that is history in the making.

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:19.240
Yes, this is the place on the internet where
we get unreasonably excited by a large blue

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:20.340
bar.

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:22.230
Welcome to Two Minute Papers!

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:24.780
But what does this really mean?

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:32.200
Well, in absolute terms, AlphaFold 2 is considered
to be about three times better than previous

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:33.200
solutions.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:36.780
And all that in just two years.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:40.270
That is a miracle right in front of our eyes.

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:46.690
Now, let’s pop the hood, and see what is
inside this AI, and...hmm!

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:50.290
Look at all these elements in the system that
make this happen.

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:52.330
So, where do we start?

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:55.570
Which of these is the most important?

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:56.970
What is the key?

00:06:56.970 --> 00:07:00.280
Well, everything…and nothing.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:02.620
I will explain this in a moment.

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:07.640
That does not sound very enlightening, so,
what is going on?

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:14.380
DeepMind ran a detailed ablation study on
what mattered and the result is the following:

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:15.730
everything mattered.

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:16.730
Look.

00:07:16.730 --> 00:07:22.980
With few exceptions, every part adds its own
little piece to the final result, but, none

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:25.540
of these techniques are a silver bullet.

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:32.490
But to understand a bit more about what is
going on here, let’s look at three things.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:38.870
One, AlphaFold 2 is an end to end network
that can perform iterative refinement.

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:40.190
What do these mean?

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:45.800
What this means is that everything needed
to solve the task is learned by the network,

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:51.380
and that it starts out from a rough initial
guess, and then, it gradually improves it.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:57.570
You see this process here, and it truly is
a sight to behold.

00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:00.250
Two, it uses an attention-based model.

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:01.680
What does that mean?

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.280
Well, look!

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:05.870
This is a convolutional neural network.

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:11.500
This is wired in a way that information flows
to neighboring neurons.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:17.760
This is great for image recognition, because
usually, the required information is located

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:19.360
nearby.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:24.590
For instance, let’s imagine that we wish
to train a neural network that can recognize

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:25.960
a dog.

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:28.070
What do we need to look at?

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:35.789
Floppy ears, black snout, fur, okay, we’re
good, we can conclude that we have a dog here.

00:08:35.789 --> 00:08:37.659
Now, have you noticed?

00:08:37.659 --> 00:08:42.370
Yes, all of this information is&nbsp; located
nearby.

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:48.139
Therefore a convolutional neural network is
expected to do really well at that.

00:08:48.139 --> 00:08:50.449
However, check this out.

00:08:50.449 --> 00:08:54.259
This is a transformer, which is an attention-based
model.

00:08:54.259 --> 00:08:57.970
Here, information does not flow between neighbors.

00:08:57.970 --> 00:08:58.970
No sir!

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:02.249
Here, information flows everywhere!

00:09:02.249 --> 00:09:08.029
This has spontaneous connections that are
great for almost anything if we can use them

00:09:08.029 --> 00:09:09.029
well.

00:09:09.029 --> 00:09:14.869
For instance, when reading a book, if we are
at page 100, we might need to recall some

00:09:14.869 --> 00:09:17.310
information from page 1.

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:20.639
Transformers are excellent for tasks like
that.

00:09:20.639 --> 00:09:26.449
They are still quite new, just a few years
old, and are already making breakthroughs.

00:09:26.449 --> 00:09:29.939
So, why use them for protein folding?

00:09:29.939 --> 00:09:36.959
Well, things that are 200 amino acids apart
in the text description can still be next

00:09:36.959 --> 00:09:39.949
to each other in the 3-D space.

00:09:39.949 --> 00:09:46.579
Yes, now we know that for that, we need attention
networks, for instance, a transformer.

00:09:46.579 --> 00:09:52.170
These are seeing a great deal of use these
days, for instance, Tesla also uses them for

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:54.579
training their self-driving cars.

00:09:54.579 --> 00:09:57.829
Yes, so these things mattered.

00:09:57.829 --> 00:10:01.190
But so many other things did too.

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:06.600
Now, I mentioned that the key is everything…and
nothing.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:07.709
What does that mean?

00:10:07.709 --> 00:10:09.839
Well, look here.

00:10:09.839 --> 00:10:13.639
Apart from a couple examples, there is no
silver bullet here.

00:10:13.639 --> 00:10:17.879
Every single one of these improvements bump
the score a little bit.

00:10:17.879 --> 00:10:21.119
But all of them are needed for the breakthrough.

00:10:21.119 --> 00:10:26.040
Now, one of the important elements is also
adding physics knowledge.

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:27.699
How do you do that?

00:10:27.699 --> 00:10:32.070
Typically the answer is that you don’t.

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:38.600
When we design a handcrafted technique, we
write the knowledge into an algorithm by hand.

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:44.370
For instance, in chess, there are a bunch
of well-known openings for the algorithm to

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:45.370
consider.

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:50.170
For protein folding, we can tell the algorithm
that if you see this structure, it typically

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:52.029
bends this way.

00:10:52.029 --> 00:10:57.869
Or we can also show it common protein templates,
kind of like openings for chess.

00:10:57.869 --> 00:11:02.749
We can add all this valuable expertise to
a handcrafted technique.

00:11:02.749 --> 00:11:10.029
Now, we noted that scientists at DeepMind
decided to use an end to end learning system.

00:11:10.029 --> 00:11:14.800
I would like to unpack that for a moment,
because this design decision is not trivial

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:16.689
at all.

00:11:16.689 --> 00:11:20.770
In fact, in a moment, I bet you will think
it’s flat out counterintuitive.

00:11:20.770 --> 00:11:23.139
Let me explain.

00:11:23.139 --> 00:11:28.250
If we are a physics simulation researcher
and we have a physics simulation problem,

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:34.649
we take our physics knowledge, and write a
computer program to make use of that knowledge.

00:11:34.649 --> 00:11:40.309
For instance, here, you see this being used
to great effect, so much so that what you

00:11:40.309 --> 00:11:45.389
see here is not reality, but a physics simulation.

00:11:45.389 --> 00:11:46.629
All handcrafted.

00:11:46.629 --> 00:11:53.489
Clearly, using this concept, we can see that
human ingenuity goes very far, and we can

00:11:53.489 --> 00:11:55.569
write super powerful programs.

00:11:55.569 --> 00:12:01.489
Or, we can do end to end learning, where,
surprisingly, we don’t write our knowledge

00:12:01.489 --> 00:12:03.989
into the algorithm at all.

00:12:03.989 --> 00:12:10.600
We give it training data instead, and let
the AI build up its own knowledge base from

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:11.680
that.

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:17.619
And AlphaFold is an end to end learning project,
so almost everything is learned.

00:12:17.619 --> 00:12:18.619
Almost.

00:12:18.619 --> 00:12:25.790
And, one of the great challenges of this project
was to infuse the AI with physics knowledge

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:28.970
without impacting the learning.

00:12:28.970 --> 00:12:30.950
That is super hard.

00:12:30.950 --> 00:12:32.920
So, training huh?

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:34.490
How long does this take?

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:43.769
Well, get this, DeepMind can train this incredible
folding AI in as little as 2 weeks.

00:12:43.769 --> 00:12:45.560
Why is two weeks little?

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:52.589
Well, after this step is done, the AI can
be given a new input and will be able to create

00:12:52.589 --> 00:12:55.980
this 3D structure in about a minute.

00:12:55.980 --> 00:13:01.679
And, we can then reuse this trained neural
network for as long as we wish.

00:13:01.679 --> 00:13:06.440
Whew, so… this is a lot of trouble to fold
these proteins.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:09.730
So what is all this good for?

00:13:09.730 --> 00:13:15.329
The list of applications is very impressive,
I’ll give you just a small subset of them

00:13:15.329 --> 00:13:21.999
that I really liked: it helps with us better
understand the human body, or create better

00:13:21.999 --> 00:13:29.879
medicine against malaria and many other diseases,
develop more healthy food, or develop enzymes

00:13:29.879 --> 00:13:31.970
to break down plastic waste, and more.

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:33.779
And that’s just the start.

00:13:33.779 --> 00:13:40.040
Well, you are probably asking, Károly, you
keep saying that this is a gift to humanity.

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:43.149
So…why is it a gift to humanity?

00:13:43.149 --> 00:13:45.870
Well, here comes the best part.

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:52.309
A little after publishing the paper, DeepMind
made these 3D structure predictions available

00:13:52.309 --> 00:13:54.399
for free for everyone.

00:13:54.399 --> 00:13:58.759
For instance, they have made their human protein
predictions public.

00:13:58.759 --> 00:14:04.519
Beyond that, they already have made their
predictions public for yeast, important pathogens,

00:14:04.519 --> 00:14:06.790
crop species, and more.

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:13.279
And I have already seen followup works on
how to use this for developing new drugs.

00:14:13.279 --> 00:14:15.179
What a time to be alive!

00:14:15.179 --> 00:14:19.879
Now note that this is but one step in a thousand-step
journey.

00:14:19.879 --> 00:14:22.610
But one important step nonetheless.

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:27.629
And, I would like to send huge congratulations
to DeepMind.

00:14:27.629 --> 00:14:34.059
Something like this costs a ton to develop,
and note that it is not easy, or maybe not

00:14:34.059 --> 00:14:40.100
even possible to immediately make a product
out of this and monetize it.

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:47.029
This truly is a gift to humanity, and a project
like this can only emerge from proper long

00:14:47.029 --> 00:14:51.850
term thinking that focuses on what matters
in the long term.

00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:55.339
Not just thinking about what is right now.

00:14:55.339 --> 00:14:56.339
Bravo.

00:14:56.339 --> 00:15:00.480
Now, of course, not even AlphaFold 2 is perfect.

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:05.519
For instance, it's not always very confident
about its own solutions.

00:15:05.519 --> 00:15:09.639
It also performs poorly in antibody interactions.

00:15:09.639 --> 00:15:15.459
Both of these are subject to intense scrutiny
and followup papers are already appearing

00:15:15.459 --> 00:15:17.110
in these directions.

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:19.480
Now, one last thing.

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:21.899
Why does this video exist?

00:15:21.899 --> 00:15:28.350
I got a lot of questions from you asking why
I made no video on AlphaFold.

00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:35.660
Well, protein folding is a highly multidisciplinary
problem, which, beyond machine learning, requires

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:38.829
tons of knowledge in biology, physics, engineering.

00:15:38.829 --> 00:15:47.559
And my answer was that I don’t feel qualified
to speak about this project, so I better not.

00:15:47.559 --> 00:15:50.550
However, something has changed.

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:51.760
What has changed?

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:57.269
Well, now I had the help of someone who is
very qualified.

00:15:57.269 --> 00:16:03.480
As qualified as it gets, because it is the
one and only John Jumper, the first author

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:09.089
of the paper, who kindly agreed to review
the contents of this video to make sure that

00:16:09.089 --> 00:16:11.319
I did not mess up too badly.

00:16:11.319 --> 00:16:17.240
Thus, I would like to send a big thank you
to John, his team, and DeepMind, for creating

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:21.949
AlphaFold, and helping this video to come
into existence.

00:16:21.949 --> 00:16:27.690
It came late, so we missed out on a ton of
views, but that doesn't matter.

00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:34.529
What matters is that you get an easy to understand
and accurate description of AlphaFold.

00:16:34.529 --> 00:17:36.000
Thank you so much for your patience!

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:37.900
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

